                "To preserve remarketing capabilities without compromising consumer privacy, the Privacy Sandbox engineered the 'Protected Audience API' (formerly FLEDGE: First Locally-Executed Decision over Groups Experiment). The Protected Audience API represents one of the most sophisticated distributed computing systems ever deployed in web technology: it relocates the entire real-time ad auction from cloud servers into isolated, sandboxed execution worklets running directly inside the consumer's web browser!",
                "The Protected Audience API architecture is structured across three fundamental roles: (1) 'Interest Group Owners' (Demand-Side Platforms or Advertisers) who curate audience cohorts; (2) 'Auction Sellers' (Supply-Side Platforms or Publishers) who host and monetize ad inventory; and (3) 'The Browser Client' which acts as the neutral, isolated auctioneer. The lifecycle unfolds across three operational phases: Phase 1: 'Joining an Interest Group'—when a consumer browses a merchant's website (e.g., viewing luxury luggage on travelbrand.com), the merchant's script calls navigator.joinAdInterestGroup(). The browser creates a persistent local record containing: the interest group owner domain, the group name (e.g., 'luxury-carry-on-viewers'), bidding script URLs, and trusted bidding signals keys. Crucially, this record is stored strictly inside browser sandboxed storage; neither the publisher nor external networks are informed of membership.",
